In `run` it uses `ctx.model().binding(&reference)` (line 132) to consult the semantic model — this is how you'd verify a `.length` isn't a shadowed local, or resolve globals. The `action` matches on the enum variant and does two different `replace_node` mutations (lines 162-193). Note `#[test] fn test_order()` at the bottom — rules can carry unit tests inline.

For `useExplicitLengthCheck` (which flips `foo.length` / `!foo.length` / `foo.length > 0` in boolean contexts): pattern (a) is the closer model — `Ast<...>` query on a member/unary/binary expression plus a factory-built replacement. Use `Semantic<...>` only if you need to resolve bindings.

